            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Spiritual fruitfulness, covenant blessing, and the blood of Christ (the Eucharist)\",\"primary_basis\":[\"John 15:1-8\",\"Matthew 26:27-29\",\"Numbers 13:23\",\"Genesis 49:11\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Divine judgment and God's wrath (when harvested and crushed in a winepress)\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 14:18-20\",\"Isaiah 63:2-3\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Israel's rebellion, spiritual failure, and sin (when depicted as wild or sour grapes)\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 5:2-4\",\"Jeremiah 31:29\",\"Ezekiel 18:2\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Grapes are a highly multivalent symbol in Christian interpretation, heavily dependent on context. Good grapes or the 'fruit of the vine' typically represent divine blessing, spiritual fruitfulness, and the blood of the New Covenant. Conversely, trampled grapes symbolize severe divine judgment, and sour or wild grapes signify apostasy and moral failure.\"}",
